    {
      "id": "graph-native-chemistry-core",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Core Idea",
      "root": false,
      "position": {"x": -250, "y": -260},
      "width": 560,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {"id": "root", "label": "root", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "any", "angle": 210},
        {"id": "top", "label": "top", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 270},
        {"id": "right", "label": "right", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 0},
        {"id": "bottom", "label": "bottom", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 90},
        {"id": "left", "label": "left", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 180}
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {"background": "#ecfeff", "border": "3px solid #0891b2", "borderRadius": 24},
      "data": {"markdown": "# 🧪 Chemistry as a graph system\n\nBuild a **canonical library of element graphs** instead of a single static periodic table.\n\nThe familiar periodic table becomes one **view** over those reusable resources. Other layouts can reorganize the same elements by electron structure, category, state, abundance, discovery era, or other properties.\n\nThen go deeper: in molecule graphs, **edges carry chemistry**. Single, double, triple, aromatic, ionic, hydrogen, and coordinate bonds can become semantic edge types.", "visibilityRole": "browser"}
    },
    {
      "id": "graph-native-chemistry-elements",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Canonical Element Library",
      "root": false,
      "position": {"x": -920, "y": 180},
      "width": 470,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {"id": "root", "label": "root", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "any", "angle": 210},
        {"id": "top", "label": "top", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 270},
        {"id": "right", "label": "right", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 0},
        {"id": "bottom", "label": "bottom", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 90},
        {"id": "left", "label": "left", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 180}
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {"background": "#f0fdfa", "border": "3px solid #14b8a6", "borderRadius": 22},
      "data": {"markdown": "# ⚛️ Canonical element graphs\n\nCreate one reusable graph per element with atomic number, symbol, mass, electron configuration, group, period, phase, melting/boiling points, electronegativity, isotopes, history, uses, hazards, and related chemistry.\n\n**The element is the durable resource. Tables merely reference it.**", "visibilityRole": "browser"}
    },
    {
      "id": "graph-native-chemistry-views",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Alternative Periodic Views",
      "root": false,
      "position": {"x": -300, "y": 180},
      "width": 500,
      "height": 350,
      "ports": [
        {"id": "root", "label": "root", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "any", "angle": 210},
        {"id": "top", "label": "top", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 270},
        {"id": "right", "label": "right", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 0},
        {"id": "bottom", "label": "bottom", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 90},
        {"id": "left", "label": "left", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 180}
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {"background": "#eef2ff", "border": "3px solid #6366f1", "borderRadius": 22},
      "data": {"markdown": "# 🧭 Periodic tables become views\n\nThe standard classroom layout is only one projection. The same element resources could drive:\n\n- standard period/group table\n- category clusters\n- s / p / d / f blocks\n- state-at-room-temperature view\n- electronegativity or atomic-radius heatmaps\n- abundance or discovery timelines\n- industrial / biological relevance\n- left-step, spiral, circular, or other alternative tables\n\n**Same elements. Different organizing logic.**", "visibilityRole": "browser"}
    },
    {
      "id": "graph-native-chemistry-bonds",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Molecules and Typed Bonds",
      "root": false,
      "position": {"x": 340, "y": 160},
      "width": 500,
      "height": 360,
      "ports": [
        {"id": "root", "label": "root", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "any", "angle": 210},
        {"id": "top", "label": "top", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 270},
        {"id": "right", "label": "right", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 0},
        {"id": "bottom", "label": "bottom", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 90},
        {"id": "left", "label": "left", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 180}
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {"background": "#fff7ed", "border": "3px solid #f97316", "borderRadius": 22},
      "data": {"markdown": "# 🔗 Molecules are graphs\n\nAtom or element instances become nodes. **Chemical bonds become typed edges.**\n\n- single bond · order 1 · one line\n- double bond · order 2 · two lines\n- triple bond · order 3 · three lines\n- aromatic, ionic, hydrogen, coordinate / dative interactions\n\nThe edge stops being decoration. Its semantics and presentation become part of the scientific model. From there, reaction graphs can add reactants, products, catalysts, conditions, intermediates, and transformations.", "visibilityRole": "browser"}
    },
    {
      "id": "graph-native-chemistry-next",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Prototype",
      "root": false,
      "position": {"x": -300, "y": 680},
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {"id": "root", "label": "root", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "any", "angle": 210},
        {"id": "top", "label": "top", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 270},
        {"id": "right", "label": "right", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 0},
        {"id": "bottom", "label": "bottom", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 90},
        {"id": "left", "label": "left", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "value", "angle": 180}
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {"background": "#f0fdf4", "border": "3px solid #16a34a", "borderRadius": 22},
      "data": {"markdown": "# 🛠️ First prototype\n\n1. Build 5–10 recognizable element graphs.\n2. Make a mini periodic-table view linking to them.\n3. Make a few molecule graphs using single, double, and triple bond edges.\n4. Add one alternate periodic view using the same element resources.\n5. Use the exercise to harden reusable references, typed edge semantics, and edge presentation.\n\nIf this works, all 118 elements become mostly data and automation.", "visibilityRole": "browser"}
    }
  ],
